Another promising library that could do validation and type inference out of the box would be [Typia|https://www.npmjs.com/package/typia]. It could also replace all our custom validation types by using comment tags directly on the interfaces. Although they protect against some issues (like swapping by mistake city_id with courier_id) custom validation types are easily confused with real types when in fact they are just aliases used for transferring information to the typescript parser to generate validators which imho is a bad direction. They also lose meaning during operations, for example:

{noformat}const a = 4 as tPositiveNumber;
const b = 3 as tPositiveNumber;
const c: number = b - a; 
// will be a number and 
// if you try to use "as tPositiveNumber" you'd also be dead wrong{noformat}

